---
import Chapter from '../Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../Figure.astro';
import FigCapabilities from '../figures/FigCapabilities.astro';

const removed = [
  ['The bridge CA', 'The federation falls: there is no path between domains.'],
  ['The cross-certificates', 'The federation falls: the hierarchies are isolated again.'],
  ['The trust list', 'Verifiers are left without anchors.'],
  [
    'PolarisChain',
    'Nothing in the federation falls. Certificates stay signed and any RFC 5280 validator still works. What is lost is knowing what each certificate enables, and choosing among the paths.',
  ],
];

const caps = [
  {
    name: 'Govern',
    cls: 'govern',
    text: 'Before a cross-certificate is signed, or a policy changes: which anchors gain or lose an admissible path, and which selected paths change.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Discover',
    cls: 'discover',
    text: 'Inside the handshake: whether an admissible path exists from an anchor the receiver accepts and, if there are several, which one it prefers.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Audit',
    cls: 'audit',
    text: 'After the decision: not another verdict but its reason, the constraint or missing edge behind a rejection, with a record that reproduces it.',
  },
];
---

<Chapter id="proposal" num="04" kicker="The proposal" title="A frame of reference, not another federation.">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Polaris is not on the ship's route. It is a fixed reference outside it, which lets navigators
      compute their own position. PolarisChain plays the same role: <strong>it establishes no trust
      relationship</strong>. It reads the ones that others established by issuing certificates,
      represents them as a graph and computes over them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="key">It does not establish trust. It computes over the trust that already exists.</p>

  <table class="removed">
    <caption class="label">Remove each component and watch what falls</caption>
    <tbody>
      {
        removed.map(([what, effect], i) => (
          <tr class={i === removed.length - 1 ? 'is-us' : ''}>
            <th scope="row">{what}</th>
            <td>{effect}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="prose after">
    <p>
      From that single object follow three computable capabilities. They are not three tools that
      share data, but <strong>three questions put to the same graph</strong>, at different moments
      and by different people.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure num={4} class="fig-caps" caption="Govern, Discover and Audit query the same governed graph with the same receiver policy.">
    <FigCapabilities />
  </Figure>

  <div class="caps grid-3">
    {
      caps.map((c) => (
        <div class={`cap cap--${c.cls}`}>
          <h3>{c.name}</h3>
          <p class="small">{c.text}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</Chapter>

<style>
  /* Narrower than the text and centred, with room above and below. */
  .removed {
    width: min(100%, 46rem);
    margin: 3.5rem auto 0;
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }
  .removed caption {
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
    text-align: left;
  }
  .removed th,
  .removed td {
    padding: 0.75rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    vertical-align: top;
    text-align: left;
  }
  .removed th {
    width: 12rem;
    padding-right: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .removed td {
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }
  .removed tr.is-us th {
    color: var(--ieee-yellow-75);
  }
  .removed tr.is-us td {
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .after {
    margin-top: 4rem;
  }

  .after + :global(.fig-caps) {
    margin-top: 4rem;
  }

  .caps {
    margin-top: 3.4rem;
  }
  .cap {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    border-left: 1px solid;
  }
  .cap .small {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .cap--govern {
    border-color: var(--govern);
  }
  .cap--discover {
    border-color: var(--discover);
  }
  .cap--audit {
    border-color: var(--audit);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .removed th {
      width: 9rem;
      padding-right: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
